# No concrete config value tests

Do **not** write tests whose purpose is to assert that a config key, env default, or scheduled command list currently equals a specific value.

Config exists so values can change without code changes. Locking literals in tests forces pointless test edits on every normal config tweak.

## Do not test

- `assertSame` / `assertEquals` / `assertContains` on `config('…')` or `Config::get('…')` for defaults or tunable settings
- `schedule:list` (or `Schedule::events()`) checklists that command X / flag Y is registered
- Snapshotting arrays from config files (ignore lists, retention days, badge maps, disk names, etc.)

```php
// BAD — breaks when someone changes a default
$this->assertSame(14, config('housekeeping.log_retention_days'));
$this->assertContains('Java object is gone', config('sentry.browser.ignore_errors'));

// BAD — schedule registry snapshot
$this->artisan('schedule:list')
    ->expectsOutputToContain('media-library:clean --delete-orphaned --force');
```

## Do test

- **Behavior under a given config** — `Config::set(...)` (or env) as a fixture, then assert runtime outcome
- **Config transforms / safety rules** — e.g. staging mail must force Mailpit even if SMTP env is set
- **Infrastructure wiring that is not a tunable knob** only when the app hard-depends on it (e.g. queue connection driver the worker assumes) — prefer behavior tests when possible

```php
// GOOD — fixture drives behavior
Config::set('monitoring.scheduler_heartbeat_url', 'https://heartbeat.example/scheduler');
$this->artisan('monitoring:heartbeat', ['target' => 'scheduler'])->assertSuccessful();
Http::assertSent(...);

// GOOD — safety rule under env
// staging APP_ENV forces mailpit host regardless of MAIL_HOST
```
